WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.860
In a mouse, light can silence one kind of cell in the lining of the gut.

2
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.960
Then the mouse no longer reliably prefers sugar to a calorie-free sweetener.

3
00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.960
That cell is not a neuron.

4
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.640
So how does it reach the nervous system?

5
00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.660
Through a hormone, or through a fast synapse onto a vagal neuron?

6
00:00:26.483 --> 00:00:33.203
Before 2018, the prevailing view was this, as Kaelberer and colleagues put it.

7
00:00:33.323 --> 00:00:37.083
The brain senses the gut only through passive hormone release.

8
00:00:37.983 --> 00:00:44.263
Then a process on enteroendocrine cells, once called a neuropod, was shown to touch

9
00:00:44.263 --> 00:00:50.483
mucosal nerves. Modified rabies virus was given by enema into the colon of newborn

10
00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:54.283
mice. It infected the cells and then labelled nerves.

11
00:00:55.023 --> 00:01:00.443
In two of five mice with infected colon nerves, labelling reached vagal nodose

12
00:01:00.443 --> 00:01:02.703
neurons. That shows connectivity.

13
00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:04.883
It does not show the transmitter.

14
00:01:05.817 --> 00:01:12.577
Next, speed. Researchers grew gut cells together with vagal neurons in a dish.

15
00:01:13.577 --> 00:01:15.197
Light switched on the gut cell.

16
00:01:15.997 --> 00:01:21.617
In nine pairs, currents appeared in the neuron within 60 to 800 milliseconds.

17
00:01:22.617 --> 00:01:28.477
The cells also carried transcripts for VGLUT1, which fits glutamate as the

18
00:01:28.477 --> 00:01:33.917
transmitter. Currents that fast point to a fast transmitter acting on the neuron.

19
00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:40.807
Then a blocker. Kynurenic acid blocks ionotropic glutamate receptors.

20
00:01:41.547 --> 00:01:42.907
It abolished the currents.

21
00:01:43.827 --> 00:01:45.927
After washout, the currents came back.

22
00:01:46.707 --> 00:01:48.367
This was seen in four neurons.

23
00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:53.807
Recovery matters. It separates blocked receptors from damaged cells.

24
00:01:54.717 --> 00:01:56.497
Now the living mouse.

25
00:01:57.597 --> 00:02:03.857
In anaesthetised mice, a recording from the cervical vagus showed firing rise within

26
00:02:03.857 --> 00:02:06.077
seconds of sucrose in the intestine.

27
00:02:07.437 --> 00:02:11.797
Phloridzin, which blocks SGLT1, abolished that response.

28
00:02:13.097 --> 00:02:16.557
Light that silenced the CCK cells abolished it too.

29
00:02:17.717 --> 00:02:20.937
Light that drove the same cells raised vagal firing.

30
00:02:21.887 --> 00:02:27.417
The authors concluded that the cells are necessary and sufficient to transduce a

31
00:02:27.417 --> 00:02:33.837
glucose stimulus. Firing within seconds alone could fit a hormone or a synapse.

32
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.530
All of this is mouse work, and each step has a limit.

33
00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.570
Tracing shows connectivity only.

34
00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:43.930
Cocultures are in a dish.

35
00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:51.050
Only 18.9 percent of CCK cells touched sensory fibres in fixed tissue.

36
00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:56.010
The human data are glutamate release from organoids of one donor.

37
00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:02.230
In a study of serotonin-making gut cells, the recorded fibres were not shown to be

38
00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:06.550
vagal. A synaptic route does not make hormones irrelevant.

39
00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:13.670
Keep the rule. A fast synapse is shown by a chain of evidence, not by one result.
